# Copy to the CLIENT REPO ROOT as `.env` (the scripts resolve it two levels
# above scripts/data/). Do not commit the filled-in copy.
#
# KEY NAMING — `_ID` vs `_NAME` is a rule, not a label. A key holding a GUID is
# stage-specific and must carry an <ENV>_ prefix; a key holding an item display
# name survives dev/test/prod promotion unchanged and is written once, bare. So
# the suffix tells you whether the key needs prefixing. Prefer names where the
# script accepts them: they make this file reviewable (a GUID diff tells you
# nothing about what it points at) and they fail loudly when wrong, where a
# stale GUID silently queries the previous target. Use a GUID where one is
# required (PBI_WORKSPACE_ID) or where display names collide.
#
# MULTI-ENVIRONMENT REPOS (dev/test/prod workspaces behind deployment
# pipelines): prefix any connection key below with an environment name (alnum,
# no underscore) and pick the environment per run with -E <env> on any wrapper,
# the FAB_ENV environment variable, or ENV_DEFAULT here. Lookup is <ENV>_<KEY>
# first, bare <KEY> as fallback — deployment pipelines keep item display names
# identical across stages, so typically only hosts, cluster URIs, and workspace
# GUIDs get prefixed and name-valued keys stay bare. Point ENV_DEFAULT at the
# safe environment; reaching prod should require an explicit -E.
#
#   ENV_DEFAULT=DEV
#   DEV_SQL_ENDPOINT_WAREHOUSE=<xxx>.datawarehouse.fabric.microsoft.com/<WarehouseName>
#   PROD_SQL_ENDPOINT_WAREHOUSE=<yyy>.datawarehouse.fabric.microsoft.com/<WarehouseName>
#   DEV_KUSTO_CLUSTER_URI=https://<cluster-d>.<region>.kusto.fabric.microsoft.com
#   PROD_KUSTO_CLUSTER_URI=https://<cluster-p>.<region>.kusto.fabric.microsoft.com
#   KUSTO_DATABASE_OPERATION=<KqlDatabaseName>
#   KUSTO_DATABASE_DEFAULT=OPERATION
#   DEV_PBI_WORKSPACE_ID=<workspace-guid>
#   PROD_PBI_WORKSPACE_ID=<workspace-guid>
#   PBI_SEMANTIC_MODEL_NAME=<SemanticModelName>

# sql.sh — one entry per endpoint, named whatever you like. Two value shapes
# are accepted: a bare host with the item display name appended (Fabric
# Warehouse / Lakehouse SQL endpoint style), or a full ADO.NET connection
# string (Fabric SQL Database / Azure SQL style — only Server= and
# Initial Catalog=/Database= are parsed). Run sql.sh -l to list what's set.
SQL_ENDPOINT_WAREHOUSE=<xxx>.datawarehouse.fabric.microsoft.com/<WarehouseName>
# Lakehouse SQL analytics endpoint — same host as the workspace's Warehouse,
# database is the Lakehouse display name. Read-only; a T-SQL alternative to
# lake.sh for anyone not using DuckDB.
SQL_ENDPOINT_LAKEHOUSE=<xxx>.datawarehouse.fabric.microsoft.com/<LakehouseName>
SQL_ENDPOINT_DATABASE=Server=tcp:<server>.database.windows.net,1433;Initial Catalog=<database>;Encrypt=True;
# Which endpoint runs when -e isn't passed. Optional with exactly one entry
# defined; with several (as above), every call needs -e without it.
SQL_ENDPOINT_DEFAULT=WAREHOUSE

# kql.sh — Query URI from the KQL database detail page. One Eventhouse exposes
# ONE query URI shared by every KQL database under it, so extra databases are
# named entries against the same host rather than a second URI — same mechanism
# as SQL_ENDPOINT_<NAME> above. Values are the KQL database display name (item
# GUID also works); name them whatever suits the repo. `kql.sh -l` lists them.
KUSTO_CLUSTER_URI=https://<cluster>.<region>.kusto.fabric.microsoft.com
KUSTO_DATABASE_OPERATION=<KqlDatabaseName>
KUSTO_DATABASE_LOGGING=<KqlDatabaseName>
# Which entry runs when -e isn't passed. Optional with exactly one entry
# defined; with several (as above), every call needs -e without it.
KUSTO_DATABASE_DEFAULT=OPERATION
# The original single-slot key. Still read, and still preferred over an
# auto-picked sole named entry, so an existing .env keeps working untouched —
# but prefer the named entries above for anything with more than one database.
#KUSTO_DATABASE=<KqlDatabaseName>

# report-png.sh and dax.sh — workspace (group) GUID from the workspace URL:
# app.powerbi.com/groups/<GUID>/... (the report is picked per run with -r, the
# semantic model with -m). One key, both scripts.
PBI_WORKSPACE_ID=<workspace-guid>

# dax.sh — semantic model run when -m isn't passed. Set either key (_ID wins if
# both are); without one, every call needs -m. `dax.sh -l` lists the workspace's
# models. The suffix is a rule, not a label — see the naming note at the top.
#PBI_SEMANTIC_MODEL_ID=<semantic-model-guid>   # stage-specific: prefix per env
PBI_SEMANTIC_MODEL_NAME=<SemanticModelName>    # stage-invariant: write once, bare

# ido.sh — Infor ION / Syteline probe. Values come from the tenant's .ionapi
# credential file (the quoted letters are its field names). These are the only
# keys here that describe a system outside Fabric, and they are stage-invariant:
# one ION tenant sits behind every Fabric stage, so none of them takes an <ENV>_
# prefix. ION_TENANT is the deliberate exception to the GUID rule at the top —
# it identifies the ERP tenant, not a Fabric item, and does not move per stage.
ION_API_BASE=https://mingle-ionapi.inforcloudsuite.com   # .ionapi "iu"
ION_TENANT=<IonTenantId>                                 # .ionapi "ti"
# Sent as X-Infor-MongooseConfig on every call; Mongoose rejects the request
# without it.
ION_MONGOOSE_CONFIG=<MongooseConfigName>
# Optional — derived from ION_SSO_BASE plus ION_TENANT when unset.
#ION_TOKEN_URL=https://mingle-sso.inforcloudsuite.com/<IonTenantId>/as/token.oauth2
#ION_SSO_BASE=https://mingle-sso.inforcloudsuite.com
#ION_IDO_SUITE=CSI                                       # optional, defaults to CSI
# Vault holding the four ION credentials. The secret NAMES are fixed in the
# script (ion-client-id, ion-client-secret, ion-saak, ion-sask) because they are
# the .ionapi field names, and nb_syteline_ingest.ipynb reads the same four.
KEY_VAULT_URI=https://<KeyVaultName>.vault.azure.net/
# REQUIRED for -w, with no default on purpose. The IANA zone the ERP stamps its
# change timestamps in — they are server-local, not UTC. A wrong value produces
# a filter that looks correct and silently matches the wrong window, which is
# why the script refuses to guess.
SYTELINE_TIMEZONE=<Area/Location>
# Which SQL_ENDPOINT_<NAME> above holds ingest.Control (the notebook's control
# table, which ido.sh reads to rebuild the registered request). Optional — falls
# back to SQL_ENDPOINT_DEFAULT; -e overrides both.
#IDO_CONTROL_ENDPOINT=WAREHOUSE
# SourceSystemName to match in ingest.Control. Optional, defaults to Syteline-ION.
#IDO_CONTROL_SYSTEM=Syteline-ION
# Interpreter for the -w timezone conversion (Python, not `date` — a Windows box
# has no IANA tzdata and `TZ=<zone> date` silently answers in GMT). Optional;
# defaults to python3 then python. Set it where neither is on PATH.
#IDO_PYTHON=uv run python

# Optional. All the scripts run `az login` themselves when the cached Azure CLI
# session can't mint a token for the endpoint; set this to pin that login to a
# tenant. Only needed for an account that is a guest in more than one — otherwise
# leave it out and az uses the home tenant. An AZURE_TENANT_ID already exported in
# the environment wins over this.
#AZURE_TENANT_ID=<tenant-id-or-domain>

# lake.sh takes no connection details from .env — they go inline in the ABFSS URL.
# AZURE_TENANT_ID above is the only key it reads, and it runs fine without a .env.
